.TH swatch "1" "Slurm Commands" "Slurm 26.05" "Slurm Commands"

.SH "NAME"
swatch \- Watch job status changes streamed from slurmctld

.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBswatch\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIjobid\fR...]

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fBswatch\fR opens a persistent connection to \fBslurmctld\fR and is told
when job attributes change, rather than polling. It first prints the
current state of each job it is watching, then one line per change until
interrupted.

This is the intended replacement for scripts that loop over \fBsqueue\fR
and compare the output: \fBswatch\fR reports only genuine changes, so
every line it prints is news, and it imposes no polling load on the
controller.

Job ids may be given as separate arguments, comma separated, or both.
With no job ids given, \fBswatch\fR uses \fBSLURM_JOB_ID\fR, which lets it
run inside a batch script with no arguments.

You may watch only your own jobs. Asking for another user's job id is not
an error; that job simply never appears in the stream. See
\fB\-\-firehose\fR for the privileged exception.

.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-attrs\fR=<\fIlist\fR>
Comma separated list of attributes to report, or \fBall\fR. Valid names
are \fBstate\fR, \fBpriority\fR, \fBpartition\fR, \fBnodes\fR,
\fBstart_time\fR, \fBend_time\fR and \fBexit_code\fR.
The default is \fBstate,priority,nodes\fR.
.IP
Only the attributes named here generate output. Watching a narrow set is
worth doing for a long\-running subscription, since a change to an
attribute you did not ask for produces no message at all.
.IP

.TP
\fB\-\-count\fR=<\fInumber\fR>
Exit after reporting this many changes. Without it \fBswatch\fR runs
until interrupted or until the connection closes.
.IP

.TP
\fB\-\-firehose\fR
Watch every job on the cluster and report every attribute, crossing user
boundaries. Restricted to \fBSlurmUser\fR and root.
.IP
This exists for a sidecar daemon that mirrors complete job state and
serves its own clients, rather than for interactive use. Because its
attribute set is derived from what Slurm tracks rather than listed out, a
newly tracked attribute appears in the stream automatically.
.IP

.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help information and exit.
.IP

.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print one JSON object per line instead of columns. Attributes the event
did not carry are omitted from the object rather than reported as null,
so a consumer can distinguish "unchanged" from "changed to nothing".
.IP

.TP
\fB\-\-noheader\fR
Do not print the column headings.
.IP

.TP
\fB\-\-query\-id\fR=<\fIid\fR>
Resume the subscription with this id rather than creating a new one. The
id is reported by \fB\-\-verbose\fR when a subscription is created, and
remains valid for \fBSubscriptionTimeout\fR seconds after \fBswatch\fR
exits. On resuming, the current state of every matching job is printed
again; nothing that happened while disconnected is replayed.
.IP
The filter and attribute set belong to the existing subscription, so job
ids, \fB\-\-firehose\fR and \fB\-\-attrs\fR cannot be given with this
option.
.IP

.TP
\fB\-Q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress informational messages from appearing on stderr.
Errors will still be displayed. Mutually exclusive with
\fB\-\-verbose\fR.
.IP

.TP
\fB\-\-usage\fR
Print a brief help message and exit.
.IP

.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increase the verbosity of \fBswatch\fR's informational messages.
Multiple \fB\-v\fR's will further increase verbosity. Mutually exclusive
with \fB\-\-quiet\fR.
.IP

.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version information and exit.
.IP

.SH "OUTPUT"
Each line reports one event for one job. The \fBEVENT\fR column is one
of:

.TP
\fBsnapshot\fR
The job has started matching, which for a newly created subscription
means every job it watches. Every selected attribute is reported.
.IP

.TP
\fBupdate\fR
A selected attribute changed. Only the attributes that changed are
reported; the rest print as "\-".
.IP

.TP
\fBdelete\fR
The job stopped matching, which for a job id filter means it has been
purged from the controller. A job's final state is always reported before
its delete.
.IP

.SH "EXIT STATUS"
\fBswatch\fR exits 0 when the stream ends normally or \fB\-\-count\fR is
reached, and 2 on error.

.SH "EXAMPLES"
Watch two jobs until interrupted:

.nf
$ swatch 12345 12346
JOBID      EVENT    STATE       PRIORITY   NODES
12345      snapshot PENDING     4021       (none)
12346      snapshot RUNNING     4022       node[001\-004]
12345      update   RUNNING     \-          node005
12345      update   COMPLETED   \-          \-
12345      delete   \-           \-          \-
.fi

Watch the enclosing job from inside a batch script, reporting only state:

.nf
swatch \-\-attrs=state
.fi

Feed job completions to another program:

.nf
$ swatch \-\-json \-\-attrs=state,exit_code 12345
{"event":"snapshot","job_id":12345,"state":"RUNNING","exit_code":0}
{"event":"update","job_id":12345,"state":"COMPLETED","exit_code":0}
{"event":"delete","job_id":12345}
.fi

Mirror the whole cluster from a privileged sidecar:

.nf
swatch \-\-firehose \-\-json
.fi

